            <ArticleTitle>Wooden Foreign Body in the Renal Pelvis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e7-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.urology.2016.05.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0090-4295(16)30153-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Migration of foreign bodies into the renal collecting system is very rare and usually concerns iatrogenically implanted objects. Migration of a foreign body from the gastrointestinal tract to the kidneys is even more rare. We present a case of migration of a wooden toothpick from the duodenum into the pelvis of the right kidney. The patient was not aware that he swallowed a toothpick; the foreign body resulted in hematuria and flank pain. The toothpick was missed during the initial computed tomography urography. Correct diagnosis was revealed by follow-up computed tomography. The toothpick was extracted endoscopically.</AbstractText>
